      <DIV class=3DcontentMargins><SPAN class=3Dcontent>
      <TABLE width=3D504>
        <TBODY>
        <TR>
          <TD align=3Dleft></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
      <H1><A name=3Dxtocid0>White Paper</A></H1>
      <H1><B><A name=3Dxtocid1>Security</A></B> in SIP-Based =
Networks</H1>
      <P><B>This paper explores various network security threat models =
faced by=20
      today's Session Initiation Protocol (SIP)-based voice networks, =
and=20
      describes network security solutions based on Cisco SIP-enabled=20
      products.</B><BR><BR></P>
      <P>SIP Security Overview</P>
      <P>Cisco voice-over IP (VoIP) infrastructure solutions such as the =
Cisco=20
      Global Long Distance solution (see <A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cisco.com/en/US/tech/tk652/tk701/www.cisco.com/go/tele=
phony">http://www.cisco.com/en/US/tech/tk652/tk701/www.cisco.com/go/telep=
hony</A>=20
      ) feature a voice-over-packet network design, using SIP to provide =

      telephony services.</P><BR><B>Figure 1</B> <BR><B>SIP IP Telephony =
Network=20
      Components<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sips=
c_wp/sipsc_w0.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Figure 1 illustrates products that are used to implement =
SIP-based=20
      voice-over-packet networks. SIP-based solutions from Cisco include =
IP=20
      phones that support SIP (Cisco IP Phone 7960 and 7940), =
SIP-enabled=20
      gateways running Cisco IOS<SUP>=AE</SUP>=82 Software (such as the =
Cisco AS5000=20
      family of universal gateways and Cisco 1700, 2600, 3600, or 7200 =
series=20
      routers), a SIP proxy server (Cisco SIP Proxy Server), and a =
firewall=20
      (Cisco PIX<SUP>=AE</SUP> Firewall). These components work together =
to=20
      provide a SIP-based VoIP solution that can be integrated with =
existing=20
      telephony networks.</P>
      <P>VoIP network security includes voice-packet security, which =
focuses on=20
      application issues, and IP security, which focuses on a network or =

      transport problem. Securing a digitized bit stream is an example =
of a=20
      transport-level problem.</P>
      <P>SIP deployments in a VoIP network are exposed to a wide range =
of=20
      network security threats and attacks. SIP may be deployed in =
relatively=20
      safe environments where the network equipment is trustworthy and =
physical=20
      security is agreeably sufficient, or into a potentially hostile =
Internet=20
      environment.</P>
      <P>An Internet environment can be considered hostile for a number =
of=20
      reasons. Most important is that attacks are not traceable. There =
have=20
      never been enough safeguards and protection in an Internet =
environment for=20
      it to be considered safe, and the potential immunity to danger of =
devices=20
      communicating on the Internet make security threats =
commonplace.</P>
      <P>There are two kinds of possible threats to a SIP-based =
network=97external=20
      and internal. External threats are attacks launched by someone who =
is not=20
      participating in the message flow during a SIP-based call. =
External=20
      threats usually occur when the voice and signaling packets =
traverse=20
      untrustworthy boundaries, and may involve third-party networks =
when the=20
      call traffic is transferred from device to device, or participant =
to=20
      participant. Internal threats are much more complex because they =
are=20
      usually launched by a SIP call participant.</P>
      <P>Because a SIP call participant launches internal attacks, the =
trust=20
      relationship is defied. Usually, endpoints within the enterprise =
are=20
      administratively controlled and secured behind firewall protection =
so they=20
      aren't expected to be capable of launching an attack. Once the =
trust=20
      relationship is defied and an endpoint acts hostile, it becomes =
very=20
      difficult to identify the source of the attack and to figure out =
the scope=20
      of the remedy.</P>
      <P>Table 1 summarizes various threats including general network- =
and=20
      application-level security issues.</P><BR><B></B>Table 1: Network =
Security=20
      Issues and Their Solutions=20
      <TABLE>
        <TBODY>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TH align=3Dleft><B>Issues</B> </TH>
          <TH align=3Dleft><B>Solution</B> </TH></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Denial-of-service (DoS) attacks:</B> Prevention of =
access to a=20
            network service by bombarding SIP proxy servers or =
voice-gateway=20
            devices on the Internet with inauthentic packets</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Configure devices to prevent such attacks</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Eavesdropping:</B> Unauthorized interception of voice =
packets=20
            or Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) media stream and =
decoding of=20
            signaling messages</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Encrypt transmitted data using encryption mechanisms like =
Secure=20
            RTP.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Packet spoofing:</B> Impersonation of a legitimate =
user=20
            transmitting data</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Send address authentication (for example, endpoint IP =
addresses)=20
            between call participants.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Replay:</B> The retransmission of a genuine message so =
that=20
            the device receiving the message reprocesses it</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Encrypt and sequence messages; in SIP this is offered at =
the=20
            application-protocol level by using CSeq and Call-ID=20
        headers.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Message integrity:</B> Ensuring that the message =
received is=20
            the same as the message that was sent</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Authenticate messages by using HTTP Digest, an option =
supported=20
            on Cisco SIP-enabled phones and the Cisco SIP Proxy=20
        Server</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
      <TABLE cellPadding=3D10>
        <TBODY>
        <TR>
          <TD></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE><BR><BR>
      <P>SIP Security Mechanisms</P>
      <P>The fundamental network-security services required for SIP are: =

      preserving the confidentiality and integrity of messaging, =
preventing=20
      replay attacks or message spoofing, providing for the =
authentication and=20
      privacy of the participants in a session, and preventing DoS =
attacks.</P>
      <P>SIP offers various security mechanisms for hop-by-hop and =
end-to-end=20
      encryption of certain sensitive header fields and the message =
body. Some=20
      of the mechanisms are built into the protocol, including different =

      variations of HTTP authentication or secure attachments.</P>
      <P>Transport- or network-layer security encrypts SIP signaling =
traffic,=20
      guaranteeing message confidentiality and integrity. IP security =
(IPSec) is=20
      a popular network-security mechanism that provides transport layer =

      security. SIP RFC 2543 bis-09 further describes detailed security =
threats=20
      and mechanisms.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid2>Authentication</A></H3>
      <P>During a call involving SIP user-agent clients, an attacker =
could=20
      masquerade as a user, forging the real identity of the client.=20
      Authentication provides a mechanism to verify that a user or =
client is=20
      legitimate.</P>
      <P>In a SIP network, the authentication can take place between the =
user=20
      agent and the proxy, where the proxy server requires a user agent =
to=20
      authenticate itself before proceeding to process an "invite" =
message from=20
      it. Similarly, a user agent can request authentication of a proxy =
or=20
      redirect server.</P>
      <P>SIP defines headers that are used for authentication. The=20
      <EM>authorization</EM> header contains a signature computed across =

      components of the SIP message. This header does not change in =
transit=20
      between proxies and consists of: the nonce, the realm, the request =
method=20
      (the type of request message dispatched by a user-agent client), =
the=20
      request-method version, and the authorization type. There is also =
a=20
      <EM>proxy-authorization</EM> header, which is used by a SIP user =
agent to=20
      identify itself to a proxy. This contains the type of =
authentication,=20
      credentials of the user agent, or realm of the resource being=20
      requested.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid3>Authorization</A></H3>
      <P>Once authentication is achieved, it must be determined whether =
that=20
      identity is authorized to use the services it is requesting. =
Despite being=20
      securely authenticated, a party may not have permission to use all =
or some=20
      of the services that are being requested, and may require further=20
      authorization.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid4>IPSec</A></H3>
      <P>A framework of open standards, IPSec provides security =
functions,=20
      authentication, and encryption at the IP layer. Three protocols =
are used=20
      in IPSec implementation:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI><EM>Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) protocol</EM>=97A =
security=20
        protocol that provides data confidentiality and protection with =
optional=20
        authentication and replay-detection services. ESP completely=20
        encapsulates user data. ESP can be used either by itself or in=20
        conjunction with authentication header. For more information, =
refer to:=20
        RFC 2406: IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP).<BR><BR>
        <LI><EM>Authentication Header (AH) protocol</EM>=97A protocol =
that can be=20
        used either by itself or with ESP, AH provides a packet =
authentication=20
        service. For more information, refer to: RFC 2402. <BR><BR>
        <LI><EM>Internet Key Exchange protocol</EM>=97A hybrid protocol =
that uses=20
        parts of Oakley and SKEME inside the Internet Security =
Association and=20
        Key Management Protocol (ISAKMP) framework, IKE is used to =
establish a=20
        shared security policy and authenticated keys for services (such =
as=20
        IPSec) that require keys. Before any IPSec traffic can be =
passed, each=20
        router, firewall, or host must be able to verify the identity of =
its=20
        peer. This can be done by manually entering preshared keys into =
both=20
        hosts, by a certification authority (CA) service, or via the =
forthcoming=20
        DNS secure (DNSSec). This is the protocol formerly known as=20
        ISAKMP/Oakley, and is defined in: RFC 2409: The Internet Key =
Exchange=20
        (IKE).<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>IPSec creates secure tunnels through untrusted networks. Sites=20
      connected by these tunnels form virtual private networks (VPNs). =
Using=20
      IPSec or VPN prevents many attacks such as those described in =
Table 1.</P>
      <P>IPSec provides many options for performing network encryption =
and=20
      authentication. Each IPSec connection can provide encryption, =
integrity,=20
      and authenticity, or all three. When the security service is =
determined,=20
      the two communicating nodes must determine exactly which =
algorithms to use=20
      [for example, data-encryption standard (DES) or International Data =

      Encryption Algorithm (IDEA) for encryption; Message Digest 5 (MD5) =
or=20
      Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA) for integrity]. After deciding on the=20
      algorithms, the two devices must share session keys. A security=20
      association (SA) is a relationship that is established between two =
VoIP=20
      entities.</P>
      <P>Secure Voice and Video Enabled IPSec VPNs</P>
      <P>In VPNs, VoIP traffic flowing across different network =
components on a=20
      shared network can be protected and secured as if it were on a =
private=20
      network. Cisco technology includes many robust security measures =
such as=20
      encrypting data, restricting access to authorized users, and =
tracking=20
      users once they are connected to the network.</P>
      <P>VPNs are built around authentication and authorization =
capabilities,=20
      offering the users of the public VoIP network the privacy and=20
      authentication they would expect from a private network. A VPN =
tunnel is=20
      created as a logical, point-to-point connection in a =
connectionless VoIP=20
      network. An encrypted VPN tunnel provides network, data, and =
addressing=20
      privacy by scrambling data so that it is understood by the =
designated=20
      parties only. IPSec is a dominant standard in constructing VPNs. =
Figure 2=20
      demonstrates VPN technology.</P><BR><B>Figure 2</B> <BR><B>Virtual =
Private=20
      Network Technology<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sips=
c_wp/sipsc_w1.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>The following steps and diagrams (Figures 3 and 4) provide an =
overview=20
      of implementing a VPN for data, voice, and video using IPSec =
tunnels.</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>Create generic routing encapsulation (GRE) tunnel =
endpoints.<BR><BR>
        <LI>Establish security and authentication policies; define =
crypto=20
        maps.<BR><BR>
        <LI>Associate crypto maps to GRE tunnels (encrypted voice =
traffic uses=20
        one of these tunnels, which terminates at a dedicated tunnel =
aggregation=20
        router).<BR><BR>
        <LI>Identify and classify inbound voice (signaling and bearer) =
LAN=20
        traffic on access gateways.<BR><BR>
        <LI>Use appropriate queuing strategies like Low Latency Queuing =
(LLQ) at=20
        the outbound WAN interface to assign higher priority to traffic =
across=20
        the voice tunnel.<BR><BR></LI></UL><BR><B>Figure 3</B> =
<BR><B>Implementing=20
      a Multiservice VPN using IPSec Tunnels<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sips=
c_wp/sipsc_w2.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B><BR><B>Figure 4</B> <BR><B>Public Domain=20
      Network<BR><BR><IMG=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.cisco.com/warp/public/cc/techno/tyvdve/sip/prodlit/sips=
c_wp/sipsc_w3.gif"=20
      align=3Dtop><BR></B>
      <P>Firewalls</P>
      <P>Endpoints such as SIP-based IP phones or other SIP-enabled =
devices in=20
      the enterprise private network that communicate with network =
components=20
      can be protected and secured by using firewalls. VoIP traffic can =
be=20
      routed to flow through policy-enforcement devices such as a =
firewall,=20
      where the traffic can be inspected before continuing.</P>
      <P>A firewall is a part of a security policy in the VoIP network =
designed=20
      to safeguard the privacy of the enterprise or service-provider =
network.=20
      Firewalls allow administrators a single point of administration =
and=20
      control to police and filter traffic that keeps unauthorized users =
such as=20
      hackers or intruders from breaking in or leaving the protected =
network.=20
      Firewalls simplify security management=97network security is =
consolidated on=20
      the firewall systems rather than having every SIP endpoint =
configured for=20
      security policies. A firewall also helps conserve precious IP=20
      addresses.</P>
      <P>Since thousands of Ipv4 addresses are registered each day and =
only a=20
      finite quantity of them are available to us, we are running out of =
public=20
      or globally recognizable Internet addresses. With this emerging =
space=20
      crisis in IP address domains, a firewall is a logical place to =
deploy=20
      Network Address Translation (NAT) to help alleviate this shortage. =
The=20
      Cisco PIX Firewall is implemented to serve both firewall and NAT=20
      functions.</P>
      <P>Implementing Security Features on Cisco SIP-Enabled VoIP =
Products</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid5>Cisco SIP Proxy Server</A></H3>
      <P>The Cisco SIP Proxy Server supports authentication, =
authorization, and=20
      IPSec. A Cisco SIP Proxy Server can be configured to provide=20
      authentication either at an external RADIUS server or at the proxy =
itself.=20
      The proxy supports three types of authentication mechanisms in =
conjunction=20
      with the appropriate RADIUS server:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol (CHAP) Password=20
        Authentication<BR><BR>
        <LI>HTTP Digest Authentication<BR><BR>
        <LI>HTTP Basic Authentication<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>CHAP-Password Authentication depends upon a "secret" key known =
only to=20
      the authenticator and that peer. CHAP provides protection against =
playback=20
      attack by the peer through the use of an incrementally changing =
identifier=20
      and a variable challenge value. The use of repeated challenges is =
intended=20
      to limit the time of exposure to any single attack.</P>
      <P>The proxy supports HTTP Digest Authentication and HTTP Basic=20
      Authentication. Both authentication mechanisms are performed as =
described=20
      in RFC 2617. RADIUS is an Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) =
protocol=20
      that functions by exchanging attribute value pairs (AV pairs) =
between the=20
      client and the server. In conjunction with an external RADIUS =
server,=20
      CHAP-Password Authentication is supported. For example, a Cisco =
SIP Proxy=20
      Server acting as a RADIUS client exchanges AV pairs with a RADIUS =
server=20
      to provide authentication functions.</P>
      <P>Using HTTP Digest Authentication, the password is never sent =
across the=20
      network in cleartext, but is always transmitted as an MD5 digest =
of the=20
      user's password. In this way, sniffing traffic on the network =
cannot=20
      identify the password.</P>
      <P>For RADIUS-supported authentication, the SIP user-agent client =
(UAC)=20
      password is stored at the RADIUS server. For proxy-supported=20
      authentication, the UAC password is stored in a subscriber table =
in a=20
      MySQL database.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid6>Authentication</A></H3>
      <P>The default authentication scheme is the HTTP Digest =
Authentication=20
      performed at the Cisco SIP Proxy Server. Though the Cisco SIP =
Proxy Server=20
      supports HTTP Basic Authentication, it is not recommended as a =
secure=20
      authentication mechanism. The authentication mechanisms performed =
at the=20
      proxy use the user name as found in the authorization header or =
the=20
      proxy-authorization header as the key to query the MySQL =
database.</P>
      <P>If the RADIUS server is used to perform authentication, the =
user name=20
      is passed as one of the AV pairs from the Cisco SIP Proxy Server =
to the=20
      RADIUS server, where it can be used to key the user search before=20
      performing authentication.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid7>Authorization</A></H3>
      <P>A user that has been authenticated is automatically authorized; =
there=20
      is no additional authorization step performed at the Cisco SIP =
Proxy=20
      Server.</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid8>Access Lists</A></H3>
      <P>The Cisco SIP Proxy Server Access Control directive in the=20
      configuration file determines access to the server. Access can be =
granted=20
      or denied based on a variety of criteria. The Allow and Deny =
directives in=20
      the Cisco SIP Proxy Server configuration file allow or deny access =
to the=20
      server based on host name or host address of the client.</P>
      <UL>
        <LI><EM>Allow</EM>=97Determines which hosts can access an area =
of the=20
        server. Access can be controlled by hostname, IP address, IP =
address=20
        range, or by other characteristics of the client request =
captured in=20
        environment variables. <BR><BR>
        <LI>The first argument to this directive is always the "from" =
hostname.=20
        The subsequent arguments can take two different forms ("all" and =

        "host"). If "Allow from all" is specified, all hosts are allowed =
access.=20
        This is subject to the configuration of the Deny and Order =
directives=20
        (see below). To allow only particular hosts or groups of hosts =
to access=20
        the server, the host can be specified in any of the following=20
        formats:<BR><BR>
        <UL>
          <LI>A (partial) domain name <BR><BR></LI></UL>
        <DL>
          <DD>Example: <B>Allow from =
company.com</B><BR></DD></DL></LI></UL>
      <P>In this example, hosts whose names match or end in this string =
are=20
      allowed access.</P>
      <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">
      <UL>
        <LI>A full IP address <BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <DL>
        <DD>Example: <B>Allow from 10.1.2.3</B><BR></DD></DL></DIV>
      <P>In this example, an IP address of a host is allowed access.</P>
      <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">
      <UL>
        <LI>A partial IP address <BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <DL>
        <DD>Example: <B>Allow from 10.1</B><BR></DD></DL></DIV>
      <P>This example shows the first 1 to 3 bytes of an IP address for =
subnet=20
      restriction.</P>
      <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">
      <UL>
        <LI>A network/netmask pair <BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <DL>
        <DD>Example: <B>Allow from =
10.1.0.0/255.255.0.0</B><BR></DD></DL></DIV>
      <P>This example shows a network a.b.c.d, and a netmask w.x.y.z. =
for finer=20
      subnet restriction.</P>
      <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">
      <UL>
        <LI>A network/nnn Classless Inter-Domain routing (CIDR)=20
        specification<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <DL>
        <DD>Example: <B>Allow from 10.1.0.0/16</B><BR></DD></DL></DIV>
      <P>This example is the same as the previous example, except the =
netmask=20
      consists of nnn high-order 1 bits.</P>
      <UL>
        <LI><EM>Deny</EM>=97Allows access to the server with =
restrictions based on=20
        hostname, IP address, or environment variables. The arguments =
for the=20
        Deny directive are identical to the arguments for the Allow =
directive.=20
        <BR><BR>
        <LI><EM>Satisfy</EM>=97Determines access policy for both types =
of access=20
        control (Allow and Deny) and authentication checks. =
<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <DL>
        <DD>The parameter can be either "all" or "any." If "all" is =
specified,=20
        the sending host should be "allowed" and authenticated. If "any" =
is=20
        specified, the sending host can be granted access if it passes =
either=20
        the access control "allow" or authentication check. In either =
case, the=20
        authentication module must be turned on.<BR><BR></DD></DL>
      <UL>
        <LI><EM>Order</EM>=97Controls the default access state and the =
order in=20
        which Allow and Deny directives are evaluated. Valid orders are: =

        <BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>&nbsp;=96 Deny, Allow=97The Deny directives are evaluated =
before the Allow=20
      directives. Access is allowed by default. Any client that does not =
match a=20
      Deny directive or does match an Allow directive will be allowed =
access to=20
      the server.</P>
      <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">
      <UL>
        <LI>Allow, Deny=97The Allow directives are evaluated before the =
Deny=20
        directives. Access is denied by default. Any client that does =
not match=20
        an Allow directive or does match a Deny directive will be denied =
access=20
        to the server. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Mutual-failure=97Only hosts that appear on the Allow list =
and do not=20
        appear on the Deny list are granted access. This ordering has =
the same=20
        effect as the Allow, Deny order. The Allow, Deny configuration =
is=20
        recommended over the Mutual-failure configuration.=20
<BR><BR></LI></UL></DIV>
      <P>In the following example, all hosts in the company.com domain =
are=20
      allowed access, and all other hosts are denied access.</P>
      <DL>
        <DD><B>Order Deny, Allow</B><BR><BR>
        <DD><B>Deny from all</B><BR><BR>
        <DD><B>Allow from company.com</B><BR><BR></DD></DL>
      <P>In the following example, all hosts in the company.com domain =
are=20
      allowed access, except for the hosts in the foo.company.com =
subdomain, who=20
      are denied access. All hosts not in the company.com domain are =
denied=20
      access because the default state is to deny access to the =
server.</P>
      <DL>
        <DD><B>Order Allow, Deny</B><BR><BR>
        <DD><B>Allow from company.com</B><BR><BR>
        <DD><B>Deny from foo.company.com</B><BR><BR></DD></DL>
      <P>If the order in the last example is changed to "Deny, Allow," =
all hosts=20
      will be allowed access. Regardless of the ordering of the =
directives in=20
      the configuration file, the "Allow from company.com" will be =
evaluated=20
      last and will override the "Deny from foo.company.com." All hosts =
not in=20
      the company.com domain will also be allowed access because the =
default=20
      state will change to "Allow."</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid9>IP Security</A></H3>
      <P>For transmission of sensitive information (VoIP traffic, for =
example)=20
      over unprotected or untrusted networks, IPSec acts as a =
network-layer=20
      security protocol that protects and authenticates IP packets =
exchanged=20
      between IPSec devices or peers (such as between a Cisco SIP Proxy =
Server=20
      and a UAC or another SIP proxy server).</P>
      <P>IPSec offers two methods of key management for setting up =
security=20
      association between IPSec peer devices:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>Manual keying=97The keys are manually installed or =
configured by the=20
        administrator. Manual keying is vulnerable to attacks where an =
attacker=20
        gains control of the proxy server or the gateway, can read the =
security=20
        configuration file, and can get access to the keys that are used =
in the=20
        traffic exchange. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Automatic keying=97In automatic keying, the keys are =
negotiated=20
        between devices that form a security association using IKE =
protocol.=20
        Security associations are automatically reconfigured and =
replenished=20
        periodically. Automatic keying is considerably more secure than =
manual=20
        keying. Automatic keys can be changed every few hours or even =
every few=20
        minutes without breaking the connection or requiring =
administrator=20
        intervention.<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>All IPSec combinations have been successfully tested and proven =
to=20
      secure traffic to and from the Cisco SIP Proxy Server on the =
following=20
      platforms:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>Solaris 8 Operating Environment (using manual key management =

        method)<BR><BR>
        <LI>RedHat Linux 6.2 (Kernel 2.2.14-5.0) with Linux Freeswan =
release 1.5=20
        (using manual and IKE key management method<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>SIP-Enabled Gateways Based on Cisco IOS Software</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid10>IPSec in Cisco VoIP Gateways</A></H3>
      <P>The version of IPSec in Cisco VoIP gateways uses Cisco IOS =
Software=20
      that provides industry-leading capabilities while being fully=20
      interoperable with a wide range of devices from other vendors. =
Through the=20
      auspices of the Automotive Network Exchange (ANX), Cisco has =
demonstrated=20
      the interoperability of the IPSec feature in Cisco IOS Software. =
The IPSec=20
      feature offers the following advanced features:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI><EM>Embedded solution</EM>=97Through a software-only =
upgrade, this=20
        does not require any modifications to the network, hosts, or=20
        applications. <BR><BR>
        <LI><EM>Digital certificate support</EM>=97Cisco and VeriSign =
have=20
        developed the Certificate Enrollment Protocol (CEP), a protocol =
for=20
        communicating with certificate authorities. Several vendors, =
including=20
        VeriSign and Entrust Technologies, will support Cisco CEP and be =

        interoperable with Cisco devices. <BR><BR>
        <LI><EM>Flexible security policy</EM>=97Extended access lists =
are used to=20
        selectively encrypt or authenticate datagrams. IP packets can be =

        selected by any combination of source or destination addresses, =
Layer 4=20
        protocols, and ports. Each encrypted stream can be separately=20
        authenticated and encrypted. For example, if Alice is sending =
Web,=20
        e-mail, and Telnet traffic to Bob, Alice's router may encrypt =
the Web=20
        traffic with one key, the Telnet traffic with another key, and =
simply=20
        pass the e-mail traffic without encryption. <BR><BR>
        <LI><EM>Part of a complete security solution</EM>=97Cisco IOS =
Software=20
        provides many security features in its Cisco Secure Integrated =
Software;=20
        authentication, authorization, and accounting (AAA); route=20
        authentication; and Kerberos Protocol. <BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>IPSec in Cisco IOS Software supports the following =
standards:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>Current RFCs and Internet drafts for IPSec and IKE: <BR><BR>
        <UL>
          <LI>ESP is per RFC 2406 <BR><BR>
          <LI>AH is per RFC 2402 <BR><BR>
          <LI>IKE is per RFC 2409 <BR><BR>
          <LI>Entire IPSec implementation is per RFC 2401 (Security =
Architecture=20
          for the Internet Protocol) <BR><BR></LI></UL>
        <LI>IPSec and IKE DoS encryption algorithms, including: <BR><BR>
        <UL>
          <LI>DES-CBC as per RFC 2405 <BR><BR>
          <LI>3DES-CBC as per RFC 2451 <BR><BR>
          <LI>40-bit DES-CBC <BR><BR>
          <LI>DES-CBC with Derived IV as specified in RFC 1829 =
<BR><BR></LI></UL>
        <LI>Authentication algorithms: <BR><BR>
        <UL>
          <LI>HMAC-MD5 as per RFC 2403 <BR><BR>
          <LI>HMAC-SHA as per RFC 2404 <BR><BR>
          <LI>Keyed MD5 as per RFC 1828 <BR><BR></LI></UL>
        <LI>Remote access functionality: <BR><BR>
        <UL>
          <LI>Private address allocation as per=20
          draft-ietf-ipsec-isakmp-mode-cfg-04.txt <BR><BR>
          <LI>Legacy authentication as per =
draft-ietf-ipsec-isakmp-xauth-04.tx=20
          <BR><BR></LI></UL></LI></UL>
      <P><EM>Access Control Lists</EM>=97Cisco gateways support =
configuring access=20
      control lists (ACLs) using standard Cisco IOS Software commands. =
ACLs=20
      should be used to provide a firewall type of security on the voice =
gateway=20
      to control the VoIP traffic in and out of the network.</P>
      <P>Standard- and static-extended ACLs provide basic traffic =
filtering=20
      capabilities. Users set criteria that describe which packets =
should be=20
      forwarded and which packets should be dropped at an interface, =
based on=20
      each packet's network-layer information. For example, all User =
Datagram=20
      Protocol (UDP) packets from a specific source IP address or =
address range=20
      can be blocked. Some extended ACLs can also examine =
transport-layer=20
      information to determine whether to block or forward packets.</P>
      <P>To define a standard IP ACL using numbers, the global =
"access-list"=20
      command is used. The ACLs default to an implicit "Deny" statement =
for=20
      blocking all traffic through it.</P>
      <P>Cisco PIX Firewalls</P>
      <P>Cisco PIX Firewalls provide full firewall protection that =
conceals the=20
      architecture of an internal network from the outside world. Cisco =
PIX=20
      Firewalls allow secure access to the Internet from within existing =
private=20
      networks, and the ability to expand and reconfigure TCP/IP =
networks=20
      without being concerned about a shortage of IP addresses.</P>
      <P>With Cisco PIX Firewalls, users can take advantage of larger =
address=20
      classes than those they may have been assigned by the Internet's =
Network=20
      Information Center (NIC). Cisco PIX Firewalls provide this access =
through=20
      its NAT facility, described by RFC 1631. Cisco PIX Firewall =
versions 6.0=20
      and 6.1 offer SIP support that solves NAT traversal issues by =
acting as an=20
      application-layer gateway (ALG).</P>
      <P>Cisco PIX Firewalls dynamically open and close UDP ports for =
secure SIP=20
      traffic to flow through. Other vendors must open a large range of =
UDP=20
      ports, which creates a security risk</P>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid11>Cisco PIX Firewall Features</A></H3>
      <P>Cisco Secure PIX Firewalls have the following features:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>Firewall capability that keeps intruders out of your =
internal=20
        network, while permitting regulated conduit access through the =
firewall=20
        for services such as electronic mail, Telnet, FTP, SNMP, and =
HTTP use.=20
        <BR><BR>
        <LI>NAT services that allow a site to share one or more =
NIC-registered=20
        IP addresses among many users.<BR><BR>
        <LI>An identity feature that allows NIC-registered IP addresses =
to pass=20
        through the firewall without address translation, while still =
retaining=20
        adaptive security. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Better performance than competing firewalls. Cisco PIX =
Firewalls=20
        gain speed through a patent-pending process called Cut-Through=20
        proxies=97the fastest way for a firewall to authenticate a user. =
Unlike a=20
        proxy server that must analyze every packet at LAYER 7 of the =
OSI model=20
        (a time- and process-intensive function), Cisco PIX Firewalls =
first=20
        query a TACACS+ or RADIUS server for authentication. Once =
approved,=20
        Cisco PIX Firewalls establish a data flow, and all subsequent =
traffic=20
        flows directly and quickly between the two parties. This =
Cut-Through=20
        capability allows Cisco PIX Firewalls to perform dramatically =
faster=20
        than proxy-based servers while maintaining a session state. =
<BR><BR>
        <LI>Support for SNMP MIB-II gets and traps. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Simplified configuration and system management with an HTML=20
        interface. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Support for Telnet, FTP, and HTTP access using RADIUS and =
TACACS+=20
        security systems. Cisco PIX Firewalls authenticate users in =
conjunction=20
        with the security systems that Cisco routers support. The =
security=20
        clients run on Cisco routers and send authentication requests to =
a=20
        central security server, which contains all user authentication =
and=20
        network service access information. <BR><BR>
        <LI>Failover capability that permits a secondary Cisco PIX =
Firewall unit=20
        to take over firewall communications if the primary unit fails. =
<BR><BR>
        <LI>Support for 10BASE-T and 100BASE-TX networking. <BR><BR>
        <LI>SIP-aware ALG functionality.<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <H3><A name=3Dxtocid12>SIP-Configuration Guidelines for Cisco PIX=20
      Firewalls</A></H3>
      <P>When using Cisco PIX Firewalls with SIP, be aware of the =
following:</P>
      <UL>
        <LI>If a firewall proxy is placed outside the firewall in the=20
        demilitarized zone (DMZ) network with Record-Route enabled, the =
list of=20
        allowed IP addresses from the outside Cisco SIP Proxy Server's =
IP=20
        address should be small, thus allowing for manageable security. =
<BR><BR>
        <LI>Outside callers cannot make calls to end points inside the =
firewall=20
        unless they have been defined as an allowed =
device.<BR><BR></LI></UL>
      <P>Cisco SIP-Enabled IP Phones</P>
      <P>The best way to secure Cisco SIP-enabled IP phones is to put =
them on=20
      private, non-Internet-addressable addresses and to make sure there =
is good=20
      perimeter security for the LAN through the use of firewalls and =
intrusion=20
      detection systems, such as Cisco Intrusion Detection System =
Sensors (Cisco=20
      IDS). Cisco IDS Sensors monitor for attacks by examining packet =
flows for=20
      'signatures' and generating alarms when attacks occur. A Cisco IDS =
Sensor=20
      should typically be placed in front of a Cisco PIX Firewall.</P>
      <P>It is recommended that the IP phones and Cisco SIP Proxy Server =
be=20
      configured to use port 5060 for signaling and for phones and =
gateways to=20
      be configured to use a specific port range for media, and for all =
of these=20
      ports to be secured. Table 2 presents security concerns in =
administrating=20
      Cisco SIP-enabled IP phones.</P><BR><B></B>Table 2: Network =
Security=20
      Issues in Cisco SIP-Enabled IP Phones=20
      <TABLE>
        <TBODY>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TH align=3Dleft><B>Security Concerns</B> </TH>
          <TH align=3Dleft><B>Solutions</B> </TH></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Trivial File Transfer Protocol (TFTP) =
eavesdropping:</B> SIP=20
            phones make TFTP requests to download configuration files =
and=20
            firmware images. TFTP is inherently insecure since files are =
sent=20
            unencrypted.</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Make FTP requests through Cisco PIX Firewalls using a =
TFTP server=20
            that is firewall protected.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) =
spoofing:</B> SIP=20
            phones make DHCP requests to get an IP address, gateway, =
boot=20
            server, and so on.</P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Secure DHCP servers protected behind Cisco PIX =
Firewall.</P>
            <P>Static IP phone addressing is secure.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Unencrypted RTP media stream</B></P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Secure IPSec VPN tunneling.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Telnet</B></P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>Disable Telnet in the phone configuration file or only =
allow this=20
            privilege on network admin workstations.</P></TD></TR>
        <TR vAlign=3Dtop>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P><B>Authentication and authorization</B></P></TD>
          <TD align=3Dleft>
            <P>HTTP Digest between phones and Cisco SIP Proxy Server. =
Cisco SIP=20
            IP phones can also authenticate methods such as INVITE, BYE, =
and=20
            CANCEL.</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
